---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const internal = (segment) => `${base}/${segment.replace(/^\//, '')}`;
const programmes = [
  {
    n: '01',
    title: 'Epistemic stewardship',
    copy: 'How should authority, accountability and authorship work when machine cognition can produce artefacts more sophisticated than their human curators can fully defend?',
  },
  {
    n: '02',
    title: 'Epistemic handoff & uptake',
    copy: 'How can valuable ideas from low-authority initiators be preserved, routed toward capable stewards and developed without confusing originator credit with epistemic authority?',
  },
  {
    n: '03',
    title: 'Agalmic economics',
    copy: 'A contemporary extension of earlier agalmics: what happens to value and allocation when useful knowledge, computation and other non-rival goods become dramatically cheaper to create?',
  },
  {
    n: '04',
    title: 'Economics of epistemic abundance',
    copy: 'When candidate knowledge becomes cheap, how should scarce attention, validation, trust, interpretation, institutional capacity and realization be allocated?',
  },
  {
    n: '05',
    title: 'Human–machine discovery',
    copy: 'When machines generate candidates and humans direct, recognize, validate or integrate them, how should novelty, discovery and contribution be attributed?',
  },
  {
    n: '06',
    title: 'Innovation search under AI',
    copy: 'Building on established search and adjacent-possible literatures, how does machine cognition alter search cost, branching, recombination, selection and realization?',
  },
  {
    n: '07',
    title: 'Representation as discovery',
    copy: 'How can systems compress, transform and embody information more cheaply while preserving the structure humans need to perceive what matters?',
  },
  {
    n: '08',
    title: 'Knowledge, property and rents',
    copy: 'How should publication, defensive disclosure, patents and incentives change when the cost of generating potentially valuable ideas collapses?',
  },
];
---
<Layout>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Economics after scarcity moves</p>
      <h1>When ideas become cheap, what becomes scarce?</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Agalmic Research studies economies in which machine cognition lowers the cost of generating candidate knowledge. The programme asks what happens to attention, verification, coordination, trust, interpretation, realization and the institutions that allocate them.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button" href={internal('research')}>Explore the programme</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href={internal('lineage')}>Inspect the intellectual lineage</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Prior art first</p>
        <h2>This programme is not starting on empty intellectual ground.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large">Many of the intuitions explored here have important antecedents, some very close.</p>
        <p>Robert Levin used <em>agalmics</em> for the study of non-scarce goods decades ago. Herbert Simon tied information abundance to attention scarcity. Nelson and Winter, Weitzman, Kauffman and later innovation scholars treat innovation as search and recombination. Cohen and Levinthal formalized absorptive capacity. Grandori already used the term <em>epistemic economics</em>. Recent work on AI-assisted cognition, claim accountability and verification gaps anticipates important parts of Working Paper 001.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <p><strong>Novelty is a conclusion of search, not a tone of voice.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <p>The project now maintains a living lineage and novelty register. Where prior art exists, we intend to use it rather than rename it; where a narrower extension remains, we will say exactly what that extension is.</p>
        <p><a href={internal('lineage')}>Read the Intellectual Lineage & Novelty Register →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Epistemic status</p>
        <h2>The programme begins by stating its own constraint.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large">Machine-assisted performance can outrun the human curator’s understanding, but that underlying problem is not ours alone.</p>
        <p>Empirical and conceptual work on metacognitive calibration, verification gaps and claim accountability already develops closely related concerns. Agalmic Research uses <strong>Authorial Capacity Constraint</strong> as a local organizing label and asks how the problem connects to contribution provenance, handoff, uptake and the economics of abundant candidate knowledge.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <p><strong>Epistemic authority should not exceed defensibility.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <p><a href={internal('publications/authorial-capacity-constraint')}>Read Working Paper 001 →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Epistemic handoff</p>
        <h2>A good idea can outrun the expertise of the person who found it.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large">The right response is neither false authority nor dismissal.</p>
        <p>The contribution/authority distinction builds on mature contributorship, epistemic-dependence and knowledge-uptake traditions. The research question is how to preserve granular originator credit while routing a candidate toward whatever expertise, evidence or verification it lacks.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <p><strong>Origin deserves credit. Authority requires warrant.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <p><a href={internal('drafts/epistemic-handoff')}>Read the Epistemic Handoff draft →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell thesis">
      <p class="eyebrow">Working thesis · Displaced Scarcity Hypothesis</p>
      <blockquote>
        When a previously binding constraint becomes sufficiently abundant, system performance and value become increasingly determined by complementary constraints that remain scarce.
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        This formulation is a synthesis/generalization hypothesis, not yet a demonstrated law. Simon, Teece, Weitzman and bottleneck/constraint traditions supply important antecedents. The research task is to determine whether a useful general result remains after those antecedents are taken seriously.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Discovery attribution</p>
        <h2>Machine generation and discovery may not be the same act.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large">A model can generate a candidate while a human directs the search and recognizes its significance.</p>
        <p>Validation, integration into prior knowledge and real-world realization may belong to still other actors. Agalmic Research therefore leaves open whether current machine cognition itself “discovers” knowledge and records the narrower events we can actually observe: generation, recognition, validation, integration and realization.</p>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>A result is novel only relative to prior knowledge, not because it surprised the human or machine that encountered it.</strong></p></div>
        <p><a href={internal('lineage')}>Explore the Discovery Attribution Problem →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="section-number">Research programme</span>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2>A programme built from antecedents, extensions and open questions.</h2>
          <p>Existing theory is an input, not an obstacle. The goal is to reuse mature ideas, identify what machine cognition actually changes, and turn the remaining questions into testable work.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        {programmes.map((item) => (
          <article class="card">
            <span class="card-kicker">{item.n}</span>
            <h3>{item.title}</h3>
            <p>{item.copy}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Standards-first provenance</p>
        <h2>Reuse mature provenance machinery.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>The research Memory Palace is being migrated toward <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/">W3C PROV</a> for provenance, <a href="https://www.researchobject.org/ro-crate/specification/1.3/">RO-Crate</a> for research-object packaging and <a href="https://credit.niso.org/">CRediT</a> for contributor roles. Local extensions are reserved for information those standards do not adequately capture.</p>
        <p><a href={internal('provenance')}>Inspect the provenance architecture →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">No AGI threshold required</p>
        <h2>The transition can be studied now.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large">Agalmic abundance is not a wager on a single future intelligence event.</p>
        <p class="muted">Cheap machine cognition already changes search, drafting, modelling and software creation. The empirical question is which costs actually fall, which complements become binding and which forms of human recognition, validation and realization remain indispensable.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Applied research</p>
        <h2>Nemosyne as a testbed.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large">The arguments should survive contact with software.</p>
        <p class="muted"><a href="https://nemosyne.world">nemosyne.world</a> explores how representations can make meaningful structure perceptible without spending more computation, complexity or human attention than necessary. Broad claims about representation and discovery have substantial prior art; any defensible novelty must live in concrete Moneta/Nemosyne mechanisms and evidence.</p>
        <p><a href="https://github.com/TsatsuAmable/nemosyne">Explore Nemosyne on GitHub ↗</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Operating model</p>
        <h2>Search lineage. Publish status. Preserve provenance. Correct quickly.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Research begins as a canonical, versioned object. Before a claim is promoted as a contribution, the project searches its intellectual lineage and records whether the result is an antecedent, rediscovery, synthesis, extension, application, operationalization or genuinely unresolved novelty question.</p>
        <div class="callout"><p>Finding prior art is a successful research result. It prevents us from spending scarce attention rediscovering a wheel and then polishing it until it looks proprietary.</p></div>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button" href={internal('principles')}>Read the principles</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href={internal('drafts')}>Browse drafts</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>